    FAILED ...::test_muse_smoke_test_persona_never_forces_a_provider_on_qa
    1 failed, 2 passed, 69 deselected in 0.40s

  MUTATION C -- provider=provider_for(...) threaded into the implement
                run_agent() call
                (breaks: never honoured on implement)

    E       AssertionError: assert 'muse' is None
    farm/tests/test_step_agent.py:528: AssertionError
    FAILED ...::test_muse_smoke_test_persona_never_forces_a_provider_on_implement
    1 failed, 71 deselected in 0.50s

  MUTATION D -- provider=provider_for(...) threaded into the deploy
                _run_and_parse() call
                (breaks: never honoured on deploy)

    E       AssertionError: assert 'muse' is None
    farm/tests/test_step_agent.py:516: AssertionError
    FAILED ...::test_muse_smoke_test_persona_never_forces_a_provider_on_deploy
    1 failed, 71 deselected in 0.15s

Note on why B alone was not enough, which is worth recording rather than
hiding: mutation B (removing the eligibility guard) failed ONLY the QA test
-- deploy and implement still passed. That is not weak coverage, it is the
three independent layers named in section 3c/3d: implement and deploy never
thread a `provider=` argument into their dispatch calls at all, so removing
the eligibility check cannot reach them. Proving their tests bite therefore
required mutations C and D, which wire `provider=` in -- the actual shape a
regression there would take. A single mutation reported as covering all four
behaviours would have been the dishonest answer.

step_agent.py was restored from a pristine copy after each mutation and
`git diff farm/step_agent.py` confirmed empty before moving on.

